[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The Campus 1-to-backbone outage is not due to a physical link failure between mswitch0 and router16. The L1/L2 links are healthy: ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on mswitch0 eth0 and eth1 and router16 eth0 and eth1; both are slaves in bond0 with MII Status: up on each side (cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0). mswitch0 can ping router16 (10.2.2.1) successfully, proving the r16_ms0/r16_ms0_2 bond is passing traffic.

The actual fault is a control-plane failure on router16: FRRouting is down (systemctl on router16: “Active: failed”), and its running config is empty—no OSPF. Consequently, router16 has only connected routes (10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24) and does not learn/advertise backbone routes. mswitch0’s OSPF shows zero neighbors, consistent with router16’s OSPF being down. From a Campus 1 host (pc5), pings toward backbone networks return “Destination Net Unreachable” from 172.16.10.254 (mswitch0), because mswitch0 lacks valid routing to those prefixes via router16. Intra-Campus-1 traffic still works as expected since switching within Campus 1 and the VLAN SVIs on mswitch0 remain operational.

Localization: router16, FRRouting/OSPF daemon. No physical interface is down on the mswitch0<->router16 link (eth0/eth1 on both ends are up). Root cause: FRR (ospfd) on router16 failed after the maintenance, removing OSPF adjacencies and routes. Corrective fix: restart FRR on router16 and restore its OSPF configuration; verify OSPF neighbors with mswitch0 on 10.2.2.0/24 and with router7 on 172.30.1.0/24, then confirm Campus 1 routes are reinstalled on mswitch0 and end-to-end reachability returns. 
=== END ===